// v3284 -- SIX ROUNDS: A CLOSED FORM WITH AN HONEST EDGE, A CHAOS DETECTOR, TWO ESCAPES FROM CRITICAL SLOWING
//          DOWN, THE PROPOSER REGISTRY, AND THE FLEET FLOOR ATLAS.
//
// Prior art was checked before every round and all six were clear ground. THREE ROUNDS FOUND BUGS IN THEIR OWN
// CONSTRUCTION AND THE FINDINGS ARE KEPT IN THE FILES RATHER THAN QUIETLY FIXED -- that is the part worth
// reading, because in two of the three the thing that was wrong was the TEST, not the physics.
//
// A -- LANDAU-ZENER (physics/quantum/landauZener.js, landau-zener.html)
//   A two-level system driven through an avoided crossing; RK4 on the complex amplitudes with NO renormalization
//   anywhere, so unitarity is a measurement (drift 1.2e-5 over 101,860 steps) and not an enforcement. The key is
//   the closed form P = exp(-pi D^2 / 2v), tracked ACROSS THE CURVE (0.62% worst at v=4, 0.06% at v=60) because
//   the factor-of-two sabotage reproduces the same SHAPE and only the range tells them apart. Both limits are
//   counterintuitive and both are pinned: sweeping FAST preserves the state rather than destroying it, and
//   'fast' is set by D^2/v -- the same v=60 with stronger coupling is no longer fast.
//   TWO CONSTRUCTION FINDINGS: (1) the window must be wide (error ~ 1/vT^2) while the timestep must resolve the
//   fastest phase (~ 2pi/vT); they pull opposite ways, and dt = 0.002 silently stopped resolving past T = 50,
//   producing a 55% error that looked like physics. (2) At finite window the amplitudes are still coherent, so
//   the final population OSCILLATES in the end time (Stueckelberg) -- measured swinging +10.5% / -15.7% on
//   identical physics -- and the code now averages over the tail instead of sampling one lucky end time.
//   AND AN HONEST SCOPE STATEMENT INSTEAD OF A WIDENED TOLERANCE: deep in the adiabatic corner (P < 0.03) the
//   coherent leftover exceeds the survival itself; widening the window moved the answer -16.0%, +5.4%, +35.9%
//   without settling, so that regime is claimed only to an order of magnitude, with the swing quoted in the gate.
//
// B -- RANDOM MATRICES AS A CHAOS DETECTOR (physics/quantum/rmt.js, rmt.html)
//   ORDER MATTERS: the Householder + implicit-shift QL eigensolver is checked against closed-form spectra
//   (a diagonal matrix, and a 3x3 with eigenvalues 2 and 2 +- sqrt 2, matched to 1e-12) BEFORE any statistical
//   claim is built on it. Then the payload: <r>, the ratio of adjacent gaps, which is invariant under smooth
//   rescaling and therefore needs NO UNFOLDING -- the step where these analyses usually go wrong. Two closed
//   forms far apart: 4 - 2 sqrt(3) = 0.5359 (GOE, measured 0.5309) against 2 ln 2 - 1 = 0.3863 (integrable,
//   measured 0.4026 on an exactly solvable rectangular billiard). Plus the semicircle and the surmise's
//   small-gap suppression (0.0760 measured against 0.0682 predicted, where Poisson would give 0.2592).
//   THE SABOTAGE IS THE LESSON: a random DIAGONAL matrix -- every entry a proper Gaussian, as random as
//   randomness gets -- shows NO repulsion and lands on Poisson (0.3779). Repulsion is not a property of
//   randomness; it is a property of the correlations a symmetry class imposes.
//   TWO CONSTRUCTION BUGS KEPT IN THE FILE: the first billiard used b = 1/sqrt(2), which is irrational in the
//   wrong place -- 1/b^2 = 2 makes every level the integer n^2 + 2m^2, and the resulting degeneracies drove <r>
//   to 0.1703, a failure that looked like a statistics bug and was a number-theory one. And the semicircle's
//   density trend at the edges correlates adjacent gaps upward, inflating <r> to 0.60 on a full spectrum until
//   the bulk was trimmed -- applied to every ensemble equally, including the sabotage.
//
// C -- WOLFF CLUSTERS (physics/statmech/wolff.js, samplers.html)
//   Same distribution FIRST -- Yang's exact magnetisation to 0.0007 -- and only then speed, because a faster
//   algorithm sampling a subtly different distribution is worse than useless and speed is what tempts you not to
//   check. Work is counted in FLIPPED SPINS, never in 'sweeps': a cluster move touches many spins and calling it
//   a sweep would flatter Wolff by exactly the factor being measured. Effective samples per unit work beat
//   Metropolis 30x at L=16, 79x at L=32, 165x at L=48 -- THE GAP GROWS, which is the signature: Metropolis
//   collapses (0.01437 -> 0.00250) while Wolff holds flat (0.4280 -> 0.4113). essOf is IMPORTED from the HMC
//   tuner, not rewritten.
//   THE SABOTAGE TURNED OUT BETTER THAN DESIGNED: P_add = 1-exp(-J/T) is IDENTICALLY P_add(2T), so it is not
//   broken at all -- it samples a perfectly legitimate Ising ensemble at DOUBLE the temperature it claims.
//   Verified by running it at T = 0.9 and recovering Yang(1.8) = 0.9569 exactly. Every configuration legitimate,
//   every diagnostic healthy, the wrong ensemble.
//
// D -- PARALLEL TEMPERING (physics/statmech/tempering.js, samplers.html)
//   The claim tempering must earn is not that it explores better; it is that it changes NOTHING it should not.
//   Configurations move between ensembles, so each temperature's marginal is exactly what a bad swap rule
//   corrupts. TWO REFERENCES, each used only where it is valid: Yang's exact curve BELOW T_c, and a same-lattice
//   control run ABOVE it -- Yang's zero is an infinite-lattice statement and this lattice carries a real
//   <|M|> ~ 0.25-0.50, so using it above T_c would have manufactured a failure out of correct physics.
//   Marginals matched the control to 0.0099 at every rung. Unconditional swapping ('both configurations are
//   legitimate, so trading them is harmless') collapses the spread across the ladder from 0.7094 to 0.1421 while
//   every chain still looks healthy: the temperatures have simply stopped meaning anything.
//
// E -- THE PROPOSER REGISTRY AND KNOB LICENCE (physics/proposers.mjs)
//   Generalizes the shape the HMC tuner and the budget allocator arrived at independently, before a third
//   instrument invents a slightly different one: propose sees the data and the reward and NEVER a verdict;
//   adjudicate is anchored to something the proposer cannot move. An instrument with no independent adjudicator
//   CANNOT BE REGISTERED AT ALL. The licence ratchet (read / propose / adopt) deliberately mirrors the
//   update-policy tiers -- the idea imported, not the code, since installing builds and turning dials are
//   different problems. Nothing can raise its own tier; a bare {pass:true} buys nothing; raising requires a
//   passing adjudication WITH evidence; and even at tier 'adopt' a refused candidate is not applied.
//   A FALSE-TRAP FINDING IS KEPT IN THE FILE, because the gate was wrong before the physics was: BOTH of the
//   first 'degenerate' candidates were measured PASSING. Wolff at 60 moves/sample lands within 0.0027 of Yang
//   (it decorrelates too fast for the trap to bite) and tau = 2.5 reproduces its viscosity to 1.2%. The traps
//   were fictional. Both proposers were rebuilt around MEASURED failure points -- burn-in deletion (|M| 0.9296
//   against Yang 0.9569) and tau = 10 (configured nu 3.1667 against measured decay 2.0753, 34.5% apart) -- after
//   mapping where those knobs actually break, which for tau is BOTH ends and not the top.
//
// F -- THE FLEET FLOOR ATLAS (tools/roundhouse/floorAtlas.mjs, floor-atlas.html)
//   Every kernel here ships a tolerance earned from a floor measured on ONE machine and doubled. The fleet has
//   been carrying the same measurement from real silicon in every submitted bench report and nothing was
//   reading it. Now something does: worst gap per adapter per kernel, and the margin the shipped tolerance
//   actually has there. The floor recorded is the WORST ever seen, never the median -- a tolerance has to cover
//   the bad day, and here the outlier IS the finding.
//   THE LOAD-BEARING RULE, and the gate spends most of its checks on it: float gaps (magmap, hmc-leapfrog) and
//   the zero-tolerance INTEGER spin count (ising) are never pooled. The aggregate an atlas naturally wants to
//   compute is precisely the meaningless one, and their average would look entirely reasonable in a table.
//   Exact-count kernels get NO margin column, because 0-or-rejected has no margin to give. Unknown kinds land in
//   `skipped` WITH a reason, since a kernel submitting into a void is the real failure mode. Not a leaderboard:
//   speed stays in perfLedger, and mixing them would rank a fast wrong answer above a slow right one.
//   THE ATLAS IS EMPTY TODAY AND THE PAGE SAYS SO -- no WGSL kernel in this lab has run on real silicon yet, and
//   the empty state is a tested state rather than an accident.
//
// REGISTRY: six new instruments (landau-zener, rmt, wolff, tempering, proposers, floor-atlas), 34 -> 40.
// Knowledge index 639 -> 645 gates.
//
// STANDING CAVEATS, unchanged and restated: everything here is LOGIC-PROVEN IN NODE. The WGSL kernels
// (hmc-bench.html, ising-bench.html) have never run on real silicon in this sandbox and each still needs one
// real-device run to earn a verdict -- which is exactly what the floor atlas is now waiting to receive. The
// fetch-cap chip still wants a cold read on Keith's rig post-v3280. The external-peer auto-update decision
// (A keep trust-on-pair / B exclude external peers / C human-in-loop staging / D signed manifests, which the
// fleet path ALREADY implements via signRelease + applyStaged + updatePolicy while the desktop peer-pull path
// does not consult it) is STILL OPEN and still waiting on you.
