// v3284 ADDENDUM -- THE PEER-UPDATE DECISION IS MADE AND IMPLEMENTED (it had been open across three versions).
//
// THE HOLE: pairing a peer granted PERMANENT update-source trust. The desktop peer-pull path fetched
// /update/offer from any paired peer and auto-applied the zip of anyone claiming a higher version. On a LAN that
// is a considered trade. Over a cloudflared tunnel it is remote code execution granted by one pairing click --
// and the FLEET path had already built the answer (signRelease.mjs ed25519, applyStaged.mjs safe extractor,
// updatePolicy.mjs pinned key) while THIS path did not consult it. Two implementations of one fact, with the
// weaker one facing the internet.
//
// DECIDED, and now enforced in ai-bridge/peerTrust.mjs:
//   LAN peer                    -> trust-on-pair KEPT. Pairing on a network you control is the consent, and the
//                                  ordinary two-machines-at-home workflow survives untouched.
//   EXTERNAL peer, signed       -> the ed25519 manifest signature must verify against the LOCALLY PINNED key.
//                                  verifyBuild is IMPORTED from the fleet path, not reimplemented.
//   EXTERNAL peer, unsigned/bad -> STAGED, SOURCE NAMED, never auto-applied. The zip is kept; a human decides.
//
// TWO RULES THAT MAKE THE CLASSIFICATION WORTH ANYTHING, both gated:
//   1. ORIGIN COMES FROM THE URL WE DIALLED, NEVER FROM THE PEER. A hostile offer declaring {origin:"lan",
//      trusted:true, skipVerification:true} is measured changing nothing.
//   2. FAIL CLOSED. Only provably-private addresses are LAN (RFC1918, loopback, link-local, CGNAT 100.64/10,
//      IPv6 ULA/link-local, .local). A BARE HOSTNAME IS NOT ASSUMED LOCAL -- it can resolve anywhere, and it can
//      resolve somewhere else a second later. 172.32/192.169 look private and are not; both classify external.
//
// ATTACKS MEASURED, with real ed25519 keypairs rather than mocks: wrong signing key -> staged; valid manifest
// with DIFFERENT BYTES -> refused outright and the zip deleted (those are not the offered build, so there is
// nothing for a human to decide about); version altered after signing -> staged; no pinned key locally -> even a
// genuinely signed build only stages; and a permissive local tier ("download") does NOT buy an external peer
// auto-apply, because verification is forced for external regardless of local policy.
//
// AND IT IS WIRED, WHICH THE GATE CHECKS AT THE SHIPPING CALL SITE rather than in the module: the pull path
// imports peerTrust, auto-apply is gated on r.applicable, a refused pull DELETES the zip (scanDownloads offers
// the newest zip to the apply flow, so leaving it would re-offer it forever), and a trust module that fails to
// load means STAGED, never trusted. A trust module nothing consults is worse than none -- it reads as a fix in a
// changelog and changes nothing on the machine.
// =================================================================================================================
// =================================================================================================================
// v3285 -- FOUR ROUNDS: FEED THE ATLAS, MAKE THE RATCHET DURABLE, CONTRACT THE BOOT SIDECAR, SCHEDULE THE SUITE.
//
// A -- THE FLOOR ATLAS IS FED (ai-bridge/androidPeerBridge.js submit path)
//   *** THIS IS THE v2973 DEFECT COMMITTED AGAIN, ONE ROUND AFTER A CHANGELOG CRITICISING DECORATIVE MODULES. ***
//   v3284 shipped the atlas with a gate, a page, and NO FEEDER: foldReport was called only by floor-atlas.html,
//   so measurements from real silicon would have sat in submission files while the atlas stayed empty -- exactly
//   what the perf ledger did for 33 versions. A module with no caller is a claim, not a capability. Folding now
//   happens on arrival, beside the timing fold, for the same reason: floor data accumulates as a SIDE EFFECT of
//   devices reporting, which is the only way it ever accumulates. The submitting device is told what its report
//   did to the fleet grading. Gated AT THE SHIPPING CALL SITE, not in the module.
//
// B -- THE KNOB LICENCE RATCHET IS DURABLE (physics/proposers.mjs)
//   v3284 mirrored the update-policy tier IDEA and not its PERSISTENCE: grants lived in a Map, so a tier earned
//   by adjudicated evidence evaporated on process exit. A ratchet that resets every restart is a suggestion.
//   Licences now persist with the evidence that bought them. A STORED TIER IS A CLAIM, NOT A GRANT: hand-editing
//   "adopt" into the file grants nothing (measured), evidence recorded for a LOWER tier does not buy a higher one
//   (measured), and opt-in expiry refuses year-old evidence because that is a fact about a machine that may no
//   longer exist. Same rule as v3284's peer round: the artefact does not get a vote on its own trustworthiness.
//
// C -- THE BOOT SIDECAR CONTRACT (ai-bridge/bootSidecar.mjs)
//   Slow startup work -- mDNS across several service types, the vault read, go2rtc spawning, a 100-entry catalog
//   walk -- does not need to be fresh at the instant a page loads. Three rules, and the third matters most:
//     1. PORTABLE BY CONSTRUCTION. PowerShell is a producer, not the contract; a report hand-emitted by a shell
//        script reads identically, so the Mac half of the fleet is not left unmeasured.
//     2. STALENESS IS STATED, NEVER SILENT. Three states: no report / stale / fresh. An input newer than the
//        report makes it stale; an input that cannot be CHECKED is assumed stale. A stale report is still
//        returned, because a hint from 30s ago beats blocking the boot (the v3272 lesson).
//     3. A SIDECAR MEASURES AND NEVER GRADES. Verdict-shaped keys are refused at write time AND at read time --
//        silently stripping one would hide a producer trying to grade, which is a bug in the producer. A helper
//        that writes {"ok":true} and is believed has moved grading to the least supervised process in the fleet.
//   Corollary from v3284: a file the server trusts at boot IS A TRUST SURFACE, so a malformed report is treated
//   as ABSENT rather than as data. Fail closed, same as the peer path.
//
// D -- GATE SELECTION UNDER A BUDGET (tools/ship/gateSelection.mjs)
//   *** NAMED gateSelection AND NOT gateBudget BECAUSE gateBudget.mjs ALREADY EXISTS AND ANSWERS A DIFFERENT
//   QUESTION *** -- how long any ONE gate may take before it is killed, versus which gates to start at all. Two
//   things wearing one label is this tree's most repeated defect and they would have drifted together within a
//   round. The per-gate timings live there and are IMPORTED: one timing table, not two.
//   The serial suite runs ~26 minutes, so in practice a change is defended by whatever its author remembered to
//   run. Reachability comes from the real import graph (affected.mjs), reachable gates are scheduled first, and
//   cheapest-first inside each band means a truncated run still covers as much as it could.
//   THE DEGENERATE POLICY IS THE SHARPEST THIS LAB HAS MEASURED: running NOTHING fits inside every budget in
//   zero seconds. So the adjudicator never asks whether the plan finished -- it names a break and the gate that
//   catches it. Empty plan refused; 2s plan refused; 180s plan catches both seeded breaks.
//   CAUGHT DURING CONSTRUCTION: gateFiles() returns ABSOLUTE paths and affectedGates() returns ENGINE-RELATIVE
//   ones. Nothing threw. The first plan reported "180 selected, 64 reachable" with an EMPTY reachable band -- a
//   plan that looked full and covered nothing, the empty-radar failure wearing a schedule. It was visible only
//   because missedReachable is a NAMED FIELD instead of a log line: 64 of 64 missed cannot be read as success.
//   And it registers on the lab-wide proposer interface, which is the point -- if that interface only ever fit
//   physics devices it was a statmech helper with ambitions, not a lab-wide contract.
//
// REGISTRY: 40 -> 42 instruments (gate-selection, boot-sidecar; both method, both page:null since neither has a
// front door yet and claiming one would be a lie). Knowledge index 646 -> 649.
//
// STANDING: the two WGSL kernels are ON THE RIG NOW -- hmc-bench.html and ising-bench.html each need one real
// run to earn a verdict, and the atlas will populate the moment they submit. Fetch-cap chip still wants a cold
