//   3.376e+11 Msun against the 5.997e+10 a luminous disk provides -- a factor of 5.6 -- and the disk alone falls
//   from 160.1 km/s at 5 kpc to 92.7 at 30, which is the observation needing explanation. Whether the extra mass
//   is a particle or gravity behaving differently is NOT something a rotation curve can settle, and a device
//   printing "dark matter detected" would be claiming past its data.
//
//   TWO EXACT IDENTITIES COME FIRST, so the claim rests on arithmetic that was checked. A singular isothermal
//   sphere gives EXACTLY v0 at every radius, spread 0.0e+0 across a sixtyfold range -- flat is what it IS, not
//   what it approximates -- which makes it the right control for the numerical integrator used on profiles with
//   no closed form, and that integrator reproduces it to 1e-6. Outside all the mass, v falls as r^-1/2 exactly:
//   v(10)/v(40) = 2.000000000000. If a halo model cannot reproduce Keplerian falloff with the halo switched off,
//   it is not the halo doing the work.
//   AND NFW IS A DIFFERENT CLAIM FROM FLAT: it peaks and declines (176.6, 213.5, 214.1, 198.1 km/s). A rotation
//   curve that cannot tell an NFW halo from an isothermal sphere is not evidence for either.
