// v3906 -- THE THREE GRADE-DOOR MODULES: TWO KEYED AGAINST ALGEBRA, ONE REFUSED WITH ITS REASON.
//
// gradedCoverage's GRADE door proposed backendConformance.mjs, blobSpace.js and blobVitals.js. All three ALREADY
// HAD SELFCHECKS -- backendConformance's even plants seven errors and catches all seven -- so the door was never
// saying "ungated". It was saying UNKEYED: every check in all three was SELF-CONSISTENCY. blobSpace asserted that
// its normal is unit-length and points out; it never knew WHERE THE SURFACE IS. blobVitals asserted that
// closestPair CHANGED from a baseline; it never knew what closestPair should return.
//
// blobSpace: the field is the Wyvill polynomial, f = a(1 - d^2/r^2)^3 with ISO = 1, so a single blob's skin is at
// d = r*sqrt(1 - a^(-1/3)) -- ALGEBRA, derived from a, r and ISO, never from anything blobSpace returns. Bisecting
// depth() lands on it to 3e-16 at four independent (a, r) pairs. The normal of a radial field must be exactly
// radial: n . rhat = 0.9999999999, which no length check could ever see, since a normal tilted twenty degrees is
// exactly as unit-length as the right one. *** AND THE GRADIENT IS SECOND ORDER: halving h QUARTERS the error,
// 1.852e-4 -> 4.617e-5 -> 1.154e-5 -> 2.883e-6, ratios 4.011, 4.003, 4.001. *** An order identifies the scheme; a
// tolerance only says today's h was small enough, and a one-sided difference would pass the tolerance.
//   THE HONEST LIMIT, MEASURED: castIntoBlob marches and stops at the first sample inside without refining the
//   last interval, so its hit is short of the true surface by 1.750e-3 against a step of 2e-3 -- ALWAYS INSIDE,
//   never on. A contact resolver trusting it as a surface point embeds every body by up to one step.
//
// blobVitals: closestPair agrees with an O(n^2) sweep written in the gate to 1e-12 -- the sweep IS the definition,
// not a faster implementation. *** AND fieldPeak DOES NOT RETURN THE PEAK. *** It is a 15^3 scan over [-2,2] and
// returns the largest SAMPLE. A blob centred ON a grid node reads its amplitude BIT-EXACTLY at three amplitudes;
// off the node it under-reads by 0.95405549519890265, and the implied offset 0.1247 sits inside the cell's
// half-diagonal 0.2309. THE AMPLITUDE-INVARIANCE IS THE PROOF IT IS GEOMETRY: a bug in the field would scale with
// a, and this does not. It is a limit of the gauge, not a bug -- but "dissolved" is judged by peak against floor,
// so a threshold tuned on on-node fixtures fires early on off-node ones.
//   I CLAIMED THAT RATIO WAS "THE SAME DOUBLE THREE TIMES" AND MY OWN CHECK REFUSED IT. I had read bit-identity
//   off a toPrecision(15) print. Measured: two of three ARE bit-identical, the third is HALF AN ULP away, because
//   dividing by 7.25 costs a rounding step that 1.5 and 3 do not. The claim is now invariance to floating-point
//   resolution, bounded in ULPS rather than percent, which is both true and stronger.
//
// backendConformance: REFUSED, and the refusal is the finding. Its content is a CONVENTION, not a measurement --
// CONTRACT is thirteen method names, and there is no closed form for "the interface has thirteen calls". It has
// thirteen because thirteen were chosen. An answer key would be a second list of thirteen names, and THE SECOND
// COPY IS NEVER THE ONE THAT GETS UPDATED. Recorded in the file in the same idiom as beam's and compose's
// measured plant refusals, so the door's proposal is not re-attempted by the next reader. The boundary is named
// too: FLOATS_PER_BODY is 7 because a rigid pose is 3 translations plus a 4-component quaternion -- FORCED by
// SE(3) rather than chosen, which is exactly why guessing 3 was a bug and not a preference.
//
// TWO OF THE THREE KEYS FOUND SOMETHING THE SELF-CONSISTENCY CHECKS COULD NOT. That is the argument for the door.
