// v3911 -- TWO THINGS OWNED W, AND THE ONE THAT TRIED TO STAND ASIDE NAMED THE WRONG MODES.
//
// Keith, on index.html: pressing W moved the camera forward AND tipped it toward the sky. Both were happening.
// Camera consumes KeyW in _moveObserver, _moveFP and _moveKaijuDrive. main.js's navPad (v1964) ALSO bound KeyW
// to "rotUp", and it did try to stand aside -- but its guard read
//
//     if (camera.mode === "fp" || camera.mode === "kaiju_drive") return;
//
// *** THAT NAMES TWO OF THE THREE MOVERS. *** It misses "observer", which is what the Camera constructor sets --
// so the collision was live in THE MODE EVERYONE STARTS IN, and absent from the two the guard did name. A and D
// were doubled the same way (strafe + pan) and S too; W is simply the one where the second effect is impossible
// to miss, because tipping is more visible than moving slightly too fast.
//
// THE GUARD WAS A SECOND DECLARATION OF THE DISPATCH IN update(), AND IT DRIFTED THE MOMENT A THIRD MOVER
// EXISTED. So the fix is not a longer list in the pad: camera.consumesKey(code) answers PER MODE AND PER KEY,
// lives beside the dispatch it has to agree with, and the pad asks instead of guessing.
//
// PER KEY IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. A blanket "the camera owns WASD" would be wrong in the other direction:
// KeyE is read ONLY by _moveKaijuDrive, so blocking it everywhere would take the pad's rotRight away in observer
// for nothing. And the pad is now BETTER in fp and kaiju_drive than the old guard left it -- that guard killed
// the entire pad in those modes, while now only the keys the camera actually reads are yielded, so zoom, R/F,
// rotate and recenter keep working in every mode.
//
// MEASURED IN CHROMIUM ON index.html, BEFORE AND AFTER, because a camera claim that is not driven is a guess:
//     old guard:  mode observer, one KeyW -> navPad fired "rotUp", pitch -0.32 -> -0.22  (tipped ~5.7 degrees)
//     fixed:      mode observer, one KeyW -> navPad fired nothing,  pitch -0.32 -> -0.32
// and camera.keys still holds KeyW in the fixed run. THAT SECOND HALF IS THE WHOLE POINT: a "fix" that stopped
// the tip by taking W away from the camera would show the same pitch numbers and break what was actually wanted.
//
// tools/ship/cameraKeys-selfcheck.mjs cross-checks the declared key set against the keys.has() literals in
// camera.js -- so the declaration cannot fall behind the code it describes -- and asserts the old hand-listed
// guard is gone from the CODE. That last check went red on its own first run, matching the replacement's own
// comment, which quotes the guard it removed in order to explain it. A COMMENT THAT DESCRIBES A BUG IS NOT THE
// BUG: it reads through sourceScan's codeOnly now, reusing the stripper rather than adding a fifth spelling of
// "remove the comments" to the four this tree already carries.
