// =================================================================================================================
// v3923 -- EVERY BUDGET IN THIS TREE IS ONE MACHINE'S STOPWATCH, AND NOTHING IN IT KNEW WHOSE.
//
// v3919 wired rigRunner to the one budget table and Keith's rig came back: assumptionMap TIMEOUT (568s budget)
// 568.1s, with the budget and its source printed exactly as intended. THE WIRING WAS FIXED AND THE GATE STILL
// COULD NOT FINISH. Measured here: the same gate completes in 234s against a MEASURED 284s. *** HIS BOX NEEDS
// MORE THAN 568 SECONDS FOR WORK THIS ONE DOES IN 234, AND NO AMOUNT OF RE-MEASURING HERE WOULD EVER SAY SO. ***
//
// gate-timings.json states the assumption in its own metadata -- "each timed individually on this box" -- and
// gateBudget.MEASURED is built from it. That was never wrong. It was never generalised either, and I quoted the
// exact reasoning at v3919 while granting androidRunner its own number: importing timings measured on one host
// applies a limit to a thing it was never measured against. THE RIG HAD THE SAME PROBLEM AND I DID NOT SEE IT,
// because on this box the numbers fit.
//
// So the host earns a scale, DERIVED FROM WHAT IT HAS ACTUALLY DONE. Every run the rig finishes is recorded
// locally against the number the table predicted, and the ratio is the scale. No data means 1.
//
// *** IT ONLY EVER GROWS A BUDGET. *** A fast box gains nothing from a shorter budget and shortening one is how
// a timeout gets manufactured out of a machine that was doing fine, so the floor is 1.0. This box measures 0.82x
// and its budgets do not move. The ceiling is 8 and it REPORTS when it clamps: past that, something is wrong
// with the box or the gate, and silently granting two hours would hide it.
//
// *** A TIMEOUT IS A LOWER BOUND, AND IT IS THE ONLY EVIDENCE A BADLY-MISMATCHED BOX CAN PRODUCE. *** If every
// slow gate is killed, no completed run ever teaches the scale anything -- and that is exactly the box that
// needs it. Keith's single killed run says his box is at least 2x, which takes assumptionMap from 568s to 1136s.
// If that is still short the next timeout raises it again: THE MECHANISM CONVERGES rather than needing the right
// number typed in once.
//
// *** AND THE DENOMINATOR, WHICH THE FIRST VERSION GOT WRONG AND DRIVING CAUGHT. *** It divided by
// gate-timings.json, the obvious reference, AND REPORTED THIS BOX AT 4.90x. gate-timings records assumptionMap
// at 47729ms for a gate that takes 234s here -- because its own capture note says a 300s ceiling was used, so
// every gate killed by that ceiling is recorded as the moment it died, and NOTHING IN THE FILE MARKS WHICH. A
// file of times, some of which are not times. The scale divides by MEASURED, the curated table each of whose
// entries carries the reason it was timed, and a gate with no MEASURED entry is SKIPPED rather than guessed at.
// A sample built on numbers that might be truncation is worse than a smaller sample.
//
// The per-host record is gitignored. Shipping it would be this defect one level up: another machine's stopwatch,
// presented as a fact.
//
// *** AND THE KILLED RUN PRINTED "(no output)", WHICH IS THE LEAST USEFUL THING IT COULD SAY. *** assumptionMap
// classifies all 108 devices before writing a line, so 568 seconds produced a blank. The tree's rule is that a
// timeout wants "either a longer budget or a smaller fixture" AND YOU CANNOT CHOOSE FROM AN EMPTY REPORT. It
// reports progress now, and the first run of it is already an answer: 30 devices in under a second, 40 at 7s,
// 50 at 33s. THE COST IS A MINORITY OF DEVICES, which is the smaller-fixture evidence nobody had.
