// v3918 -- THE OTHER THIRTY-THREE, READ ONE AT A TIME. AN IDEAL DOES NOT HAVE TO BE ZERO AND AN OBSERVABLE DOES
// NOT HAVE TO BE CALLED "err".
//
// v3917 could read plant direction on 33 of 66 and named the other 33 unreadable, saying each needed somebody to
// read it. This is that reading, and the answer was not the one the v3917 note guessed. I expected a crop of
// spacefills -- controls wearing a plant's label. *** THERE WERE NONE. Thirty-one of the thirty-three are real
// plants whose observable has a perfectly good ideal that is simply NOT ZERO AND NOT IN THE NAME. ***
//
// tomography's absoluteGain is a RATIO, so correct scaling is exactly 1 and the unweighted arm's 37.2 is the
// failure. voxelize's `order` is a convergence order, so the ideal is 2 and one-axis sampling's 0.227 is the
// failure. discovery's r2Val is a coefficient of determination: ideal 1, and the nolaw arm gives -0.105, WORSE
// THAN PREDICTING THE MEAN. gyroscope's `precessed` is the claim itself as a 0/1. And netForce, asymmetry,
// worstRise, splitDrift, differingLists, rigidDisagreements, boxExtraPairs and worstForceDiff are all ideally
// ZERO by a conservation law or an identity while matching no spelling of "error" at all. The regex was never
// the physics; it was a guess about naming.
//
// *** SO THE BIND MAY NOW DECLARE THE IDEAL, AND THE DECLARATION IS FALSIFIABLE RATHER THAN SELF-PROVING. ***
//
// plantIdeal applies the v3688 rule to any observable: |planted - ideal| must EXCEED |honest - ideal|. Declare an
// ideal for a mode that actually improves and the wall fires -- the gate drives exactly that case and requires
// the verdict `better`. plantNull is the other shape and it is a FAMILY, not an exception: stability, mpmdrucker
// and mpmpile all plant a DEAF KNOB whose observable is a responsiveness ratio with null value 1. There is no
// point-ideal to move away from, since a responsive knob is merely "far from 1", so what is declared is the null
// the plant COLLAPSES ONTO and the rule inverts. melt is the fourth: its naive arm lands exactly on
// naiveMoltenFraction = 1, THE DEVICE'S OWN RECORDED VALUE FOR THE WRONG ANSWER.
//
// EVERY DECLARED IDEAL CARRIES A SENTENCE, AND THE GATE REFUSES THE NUMBER WITHOUT IT. `plantIdeal: 0` proves
// nothing that `plantIdeal: 42` would not also prove until somebody says which one the physics requires -- the
// v3211 register of exact zeros exists for that reason and this is the same number wearing a different name. An
// ideal declared with no sentence buys NO readability, driven as its own check.
//
// READABLE 33 -> 64, ALL SIXTY-FOUR MOVING THE WRONG WAY AS DECLARED, ZERO EXCEPTIONS.
//
// *** THE LAST TWO ARE REFUSALS, AND REFUSING IS A POSITION SOMEBODY TOOK. *** v3917 left "unreadable" a silent
// bucket, and a silent bucket is where a spacefill hides -- so a plant whose direction cannot be read must now
// say so in prose, the same shape as plantRefused, naming what verifies it instead.
//
// reaction's `seeded` takes dkFromCritical 0.34456 -> 0.048136, which is CLOSER to critical -- TIDIER, NOT WORSE.
// The plant smuggles the answer into the initial condition and the winner then sits on the quantisation floor at
// every B, so the seeded run CONFIRMS a belief the real run refuted. Direction cannot read a plant whose whole
// signature is producing the more attractive number, and picking an ideal to make the wall pass is the failure
// the sentence guard exists to prevent. What catches it is the ratio clause the device already pairs with the
// observable, and the bind now says so.
//
// mpmcouple's minGap is ONE-SIDED, not a point ideal: the claim is gap >= 0 and the plant's signature is a SIGN
// CHANGE, 0.5334 -> -1.4000, straight through the bodies. plantIdeal: 0 would PASS -- 1.4 exceeds 0.5334 -- on a
// sentence that is false, since zero gap means exact contact rather than the ideal. A bound-crossing rule would
// read it properly and there is ONE DEVICE FOR IT TODAY, so it is named and left rather than given machinery of
// its own. A wall built for a population of one is a wall with a door in it.
// =================================================================================================================
// =================================================================================================================
// v3917 -- THE RULE THAT TELLS A PLANT FROM A CONTROL WAS WRITTEN DOWN AT v3688 AND NOTHING EVER RE-DERIVED IT.
//
// v3916 withdrew spacefill's plant declaration and the obvious next question is HOW MANY OTHERS ARE LIKE IT. The
// answer required a discriminator, and the tree already had one -- in prose, in the comment above
// declaredControlMode, since v3688: "THE DISCRIMINATOR IS DIRECTION AND IT IS MECHANICAL: a plant moves the
// claim's observable AWAY from its ideal; a control moves it TO the ideal." probeControlMode encodes half of it,
// requiring a control to REACH controlIdeal. probeModePlant encodes NONE of it. It asks `av !== bv`, which is
// SEPARATION, NOT WRONGNESS, and a load-bearing negative separates exactly as well as a plant does.
//
// *** MEASURED ACROSS ALL SIXTY-SIX DECLARED MODE PLANTS, AND THE RESULT SPLITS EXACTLY IN HALF. ***
//
// 33 of 66 name an ERROR-LIKE observable in plantFlips. For those, "away from ideal" is decidable from the two
// builds alone, and ALL THIRTY-THREE MOVE THE RIGHT WAY. Zero go toward ideal, zero fail to move. So the new
// gate walls that at zero rather than setting a floor under an existing debt -- there is no exception to
// grandfather, which is the only honest moment to build a wall.
//
// THE OTHER 33 NAME SOMETHING THAT IS NOT AN ERROR -- breaks, order, netForce, r2Val, moltenFraction, spread.
// For those the direction CANNOT BE READ, because no ideal is in evidence. That is not a failure and IT IS NOT A
// PASS. It is the population spacefill sat in, and the new gate names all thirty-three one at a time and
// ratchets the split so the unreadable half cannot grow while the readable half looks green. Reporting "66
// plants" while half of them cannot be shown to be WRONG rather than merely DIFFERENT is the flattering number,
// and the flattering number is the one the census has been printing.
//
// *** THE GAP IS DRIVEN, NOT DESCRIBED. *** The gate builds a device whose "planted" arm is BETTER -- shapeErrFrac
// 5e-2 -> 0, a control wearing a plant's label -- and runs BOTH probes on it. The new one returns `better`. THE
// OLD ONE RETURNS ok:true. That line is a measurement in the gate's own output, not a sentence about a bug, and
// it is there because a gate that describes the hole it closes without running the buggy path is describing the
// past. Two more driven cases follow: an arm that does not move reads `unmoved`, and SPACEFILL'S EXACT SHAPE --
// breaks 0 -> 63 with breakErrAbs zero in both arms -- reads `unreadable` instead of passing.
//
// What this does NOT do is decide the 33. It makes their status VISIBLE AND COUNTED, which is the difference
// between a debt and a leak. gyroscope's sabotage runs the wrong method and its claim genuinely fails; melt's
// naive arm moves moltenFraction with lambdaResidual identical to the bit in both arms, which is the spacefill
// shape again and now has a line in a gate's output saying so. Each of the thirty-three needs somebody to read
// it, the way the nine backlog entries did last round.
//
// Budget: measured at 534s on the run that first made it pass -- 66 devices, two builds each -- and entered in
// gateBudget's MEASURED table at that number rather than a guess.
// =================================================================================================================
// =================================================================================================================
// v3916 -- NINE OF THE TEN v3211 BACKLOG ENTRIES, AND EXAMINING THEM CAUGHT A PLANT OF MINE THAT WAS NEVER A PLANT.
//
// EXACT_ZERO_BACKLOG was written by the FIRST REAL RUN of census-selfcheck at v3211. Eight of its ten entries said
// nothing but "NOT EXAMINED". They stayed that way for seven hundred versions because the gate that would have
// kept asking could not finish -- v3913 gave it a budget, v3914 made its red visible, and this round pays the
// original debt. *** BACKLOG 10 -> 1, EXACT_OK 71 -> 80. ***
//
// The one left is plastic.creep.creepErrAbs, and it is left ON PURPOSE: v3211 measured it as a rounding
// cancellation rather than an exact construction, so it does NOT belong on a register whose entries claim the zero
// is a property of the mathematics. A backlog with a live entry in it still fires. An empty one only looks clean.
//
// *** THE FINDING: SPACEFILL'S `raster` MODE IS NOT A PLANT, IT IS THE DEVICE'S LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE. ***
//
// Seven of the nine are spacefill, and reading them properly refuted what I wrote at v3902. I declared `raster` a
// plant -- plantMode "raster", plantFlips "breaks", plantKind "knob" -- because switching to it moves `breaks`
// from 0 to 63. But breakErrAbs, WHICH IS THE DEVICE'S OWN ERROR OBSERVABLE, is exactly zero in BOTH arms. It is
// zero in raster because 63 IS THE ANALYTIC KEY AT ORDER 6: a Hilbert curve has 0 breaks, a raster has n-1, and
// the bind DERIVES the key per mode instead of remembering a constant. THE RASTER ARM IS THE DEVICE BEING RIGHT
// ABOUT A DIFFERENT CURVE. My own v3902 comment recorded "= n-1 at order 3, and the key is analytic, not
// remembered" IN THE SAME BREATH as calling it a plant, and I did not notice the two sentences disagreeing.
//
// probeModePlant passed it because probeModePlant CHECKS SEPARATION, NOT WRONGNESS -- and a load-bearing negative
// separates exactly as well as a plant does. That is the whole reason a plant has to be declared in prose by
// somebody who looked, and the reason plantRefused exists as a string: spacefill now carries one, refusedPlants()
// goes to 3 (spacefill, beam, compose), and declaredCensus drops spacefill back into undeclared where it belongs.
//
// *** THE OTHER TWO, EACH WITH ITS CONTROL. ***
//
// HEIDLER exact.peakErrFrac -- the v3211 note GUESSED this one ("presumably normalised by its own peak") and never
// checked. The guess was right, and now it is measured: the exact mode divides the waveform by its own true peak,
// so the key is 1 and the fraction is 0, and dividing a function by its own maximum cannot give anything else.
// The controls are the neighbouring modes rather than a plant: `peak` finds the peak by scan and reads 6.6688e-2,
// and the `noroot` plant reads 4.9575. A zero flanked by two live numbers from the same code path is not vacuous.
//
// MD coulomb.coulombErrFrac -- two unit charges at separations 1 and 2, graded as a RATIO so that no constant is
// being checked. It is exactly 4 with |r - 4| = 0, bit-exact because both separations are exactly representable
// and the law is 1/d^2. THE CONTROL IS THE SAME EXPRESSION AT SEPARATIONS 1 AND 3, WHICH RETURNS 9 -- so it tracks
// the exponent rather than returning a constant. An absolute magnitude here would have graded the choice of units.
//
// The round's lesson is not about spacefill. It is that A PROBE THAT CONFIRMS A DECLARATION CANNOT VALIDATE IT:
// probeModePlant can only ever say "these two arms differ", and every declaration it blessed still rests on
// somebody having read the device. Nine entries that said "NOT EXAMINED" were honest. One that said "plant" was
// not, and no gate in the tree could tell the difference.
// =================================================================================================================
// v3915 -- THE REMAINING TWENTY EXACT ZEROS, EACH MEASURED, EACH WITH THE CONTROL THAT PROVES IT IS NOT VACUOUS.
//
// v3914 cleared the two clusters and NAMED these twenty rather than registering them, because a sentence written
// without the measurement behind it is exactly what empties EXACT_OK of meaning. This round takes the twenty.
//
// THE STANDARD APPLIED TO EVERY ONE: it is not enough that the zero has an explanation. There must be a case
// where THE SAME EXPRESSION IS NOT ZERO -- a sibling observable, another mode, a declared plant, or a perturbed
// input -- because that is the only thing separating "exact by construction" from "cannot fail". Every entry
// added carries both.
//
// THE CONTROLS, BY KIND:
//   another mode        freerotation (stability reads 1.3875e-5 where conserve/flip/attitude read 0)
//                       probe.place (tour reads 1.0862e-12)
//   a declared plant    geostats (noconstraint: 3.25e-1 and 3.33e-2), galaxy (unorderedtri: 1.38e+3),
//                       clocks.crossoverErrOrder (its own comment: "2.000000000000 planted, 0 without")
//   a sibling in the    probe.transfer (periErr 0 is the departure radius the fixture SETS; apoErr 7.94e-10 is
//   same mode           the aphelion it INTEGRATES TO), mpmstress.blindfixtures (dilation blind at 0, shear
//                       sighted at 1.54e+1), kerrladder.photon (photonErr 0 at spin 0, photonWorstErr 1.24e-14
//                       across ten spin rows), xpbd.weave (anisotropyRatio 14.03 from the same function),
//                       fdtd.magic (the same at() evaluated at S=0.9 and S=0.5 in the same mode)
//   a perturbed input   seismic (bit-zero at the fixture's impedances, 1.11e-16 at Z1=1.5 Z2=7.3, and 8.73e-3
//                       when R is scaled one percent), nuclear (0 at the fixture's rates, 1.1369e-13 at
//                       l1=1e-4 l2=7), centrifuge (exact at rhoF=1, 1.71e-16 at 1.3, 1.43e-16 at 0.7777),
//                       clocks.crossoverResidual (bit-zero at four independent (M,r) pairs -- an identity)
//
// *** THE ONE WORTH READING IS beam.exponent.exponentError, BECAUSE IT IS NOT WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE. *** A fitted
// log-log slope landing bit-exactly on 3 reads like a fit against a closed form. It is not: measureTipDeflection
// is a real finite-difference solve and it is WRONG by a genuine discretisation error -- 5.21e-7 at L=0.5 rising
// to 2.67e-4 at L=4. But the ratio numeric/closed is IDENTICAL AT EVERY L, 1.0000125031215, because h = L/n makes
// the relative error scale-invariant. A CONSTANT RELATIVE ERROR MOVES THE INTERCEPT OF A LOG-LOG FIT AND NEVER
// ITS SLOPE. The zero is not the absence of error; it is an exponent being immune to the error that is present,
// which is precisely what a scaling check is supposed to be.
//
