// v3965 -- *** wg128-shared IS THE SHIPPED MAGMAP KERNEL. 1.32x, AND BIT-IDENTICAL. *** Keith's rig, Intel
// gen-9, medians of 7: base-wg64 10.7ms, wg128-shared 8.1ms, fastest of the seven, every one agreeing with the
// CPU f64 reference to the same 3.79e-6. The kernel that had "never been tuned" is now tuned by a measurement
// on the hardware that runs it, which is what magmap-bench was built for two rounds ago. *** THE OBVIOUS WAY TO
// BAKE IT IN WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE INSTRUMENT THAT MEASURED IT. *** Editing the base WGSL -- change 64 to 128,
// paste the workgroup-memory code in -- is tidier and silently wrong: magmap-bench derives all seven rows with
// variantWgsl(WGSL, v), so a base already carrying shared trig makes the four NON-shared variants shared too
// (step 4's cosT->cosW replace finds nothing left to do) and makes the three shared ones declare
// var<workgroup> cosW TWICE. Half the table mislabelled, half not compiling, and the thing that proved the
// change would have stopped being able to prove anything. So the base stays PRISTINE and the shipped kernel is
// SHIPPED_WGSL = variantWgsl(WGSL, SHIPPED_VARIANT) -- one transformation, one declaration, and "the shipped
// kernel is wg128-shared" is a fact the code computes rather than a second copy that drifts. *** AND THE
// SHARED KERNEL IS ONLY CORRECT UP TO SHARED_CAP, WHERE IT DOES NOT FAIL -- IT LIES. *** cosW/sinW are
// array<f32,64> and the cooperative load writes cosW[t] for t < P.nT. MEASURED on a real GPU at nT=96 with the
// guard removed: worst relative error 3.86e-2 against a 1e-5 tolerance -- 3864x the tolerance, no crash, no
// validation error, numbers that look like numbers. An out-of-bounds workgroup write does not announce itself.
// Every GPU caller in this tree passes nT=48, which is a fact about TODAY: magmapGpu's signature accepts any nT
// and lensBind runs 240 on the CPU. So above the cap the default falls back to the base, and the provenance tag
// says which kernel ran. *** ONE DECISION, TWO OUTPUTS, AND A PROBE FOUND THE SHAPE. *** The first cut chose the
// source with one expression and built the tag with a second that re-tested the same conditions; removing the
// cap guard then changed the kernel and NOT the label, so the run reported "base-wg64(nT>cap)" while executing
// the shared kernel. A tag that can disagree with the thing it names is worse than no tag, because it is
// believed. Deciding once makes that unrepresentable, and the probe now trips both lines instead of one.
// Verified on the GPU rather than asserted from source: the new default is BIT-IDENTICAL to the kernel it
// replaced, 0 differing cells of 441 -- "changes only where numbers are read from" is a claim about what the
// hardware does with the code. base-wg64 STAYS in VARIANTS, relabelled "the FORMER default": a bench without
// the thing it replaced measures nothing, and keeping it timed is the only way to learn that 128-shared has
// stopped being right on some future device. One device is a data point, not a law -- re-run the bench on the
// Mac and the Shield before treating this as settled everywhere.
// v3966 -- *** THE BENCH WAS TIMING TAICHI'S COMPILER AGAINST WGSL'S KERNEL, AND BATCHING IS THE FAIRNESS FIX
// RATHER THAN A TUNING TRICK. *** Keith: "Would we be able to batch the calls to Taichi to see if that would
// improve the timing?" Yes -- 17.6ms per-call to 7.4ms batched, 2.38x, and its row goes 1.33x to 3.21x. WHY it
// improves is the finding. Phase-timed on a real GPU, one magmapTaichi() call is: init 0.0, fields 0.1, upload
// 3.0, scope 0.0, COMPILE 9.1, dispatch 0.1, readback 8.5. *** THE DISPATCH -- THE KERNEL ITSELF -- IS 0.1ms. ***
// 9.1ms of every call was taichi re-parsing the kernel SOURCE with the TypeScript compiler and re-emitting WGSL.
// The same breakdown for a WGSL variant: module 0.0, pipeline 0.0, buffers 0.1 -- Chrome recompiles nothing
// between reps. So seven reps charged taichi seven compiles and the hand-written kernels zero: 12.1ms of setup
// against 0.1ms. Batching does not flatter taichi, IT STOPS PENALISING IT, and the one-off compile is still
// REPORTED rather than hidden, because dropping it would be the opposite error. *** AND THE PHASE TIMING SAID
// SOMETHING ABOUT v3965 THAT HAD TO BE CHECKED. *** The WGSL lane is 99% readback (base-wg64: 23.7 of 23.9ms).
// dispatchWorkgroups returns immediately and mapAsync is where you block for the GPU, so readback INCLUDES
// execution -- the measurement is right. And since both kernels move an identical 441 floats through an
// identical path, the only thing that differs is the kernel, so the 10.7-vs-8.1 comparison remains attributable.
// v3965 stands. *** BATCHING IS ONLY VALID IF REPEATED RUNS STAY CORRECT, WHICH IS NOT FREE TO ASSUME: *** one
// compiled kernel, one output buffer, reused. A second dispatch that accumulated instead of overwriting would
// make the FIRST run right and every later one wrong -- and the bench grades only the warm run, which is a
// measurement that certifies itself. The gate now runs it four times and grades EVERY run: 4.42e-6 each, and
// byte-identical between runs. *** THE BUN QUESTION, ANSWERED BY RUNNING IT RATHER THAN FROM MEMORY. *** There
// was no list; bun-audit.mjs covers ONE entry point (esPilot) by grepping its import graph, which is a different
// question and cannot tell you whether dgram actually binds. tools/ship/bunSurface.mjs now TRIES things.
// Measured on Linux, bun 1.3.11: all 134 ai-bridge/*Bridge.js load, ai-bridge/server.js BOOTS and serves
// /health, tools/ship/verify.mjs runs ALL GREEN, and all 14 capability probes match Node -- child_process,
// dgram, worker_threads, cluster included. The list of things that do not work is, here, EMPTY. *** THE ONE REAL
// BUG IT FOUND: /health LIED ABOUT ITS RUNTIME. *** Bun emulates process.version and answers "v24.3.0" on a box
// whose actual Node is v22.22.2 -- so under Bun the endpoint named a Node NEWER THAN ANY INSTALLED. server.js's
// own line 143 contemplates "run Node on 8788 alongside Bun on 8787", which is precisely when you ask a health
// endpoint who answered and it tells you something false. `typeof Bun` is the discriminator; there is a
// `runtime` field now. *** AND THE CAVEAT THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE RESULT: THIS IS LINUX. *** Keith's rig is
// Windows, where Bun's process, socket and native-module surfaces are weakest, and a green run here is evidence
// about this box only. bunSurface is a REPORT, not a gate -- it exits 0 either way, because a verdict that
// differs by who invoked it is not a gate -- so it can be run on the Windows box and the Mac and compared.
// v3967 -- *** node-bun.html: THE ASSESSMENT LIVES ON A PAGE NOW, NOT IN A CHANGELOG PARAGRAPH. *** Keith: "I
// thought something was not working when we selected Bun as default instead of Node," and asked for a "Node /
// Bun" page in System Tools with the assessment, the existing switch surfaced or linked, and the
// bunSurface.mjs comparison behind a button. Filed by what it drives (reads /runtime, writes use_bun.flag,
// spawns bunSurface.mjs) -- this drawer's rule, same as settings.html beside it -- and runtimes.html (the
// FLEET picker) stays separate: that page answers "which box, which runtime"; this one answers "this box,
// which runtime, and what changes". *** THE DEPENDENCY QUESTION, ANSWERED BY MEASURING RATHER THAN GUESSING.
// *** /runtime already probes eleven optional packages. Isolated, one at a time so a hang in one could not take
// the census down: Node resolves ZERO of eleven standalone; Bun resolves all eleven standalone. Through the
// REAL route the numbers differ -- Node 11 missing, Bun 10 -- because the standalone probe has no local
// node_modules at all and Bun's `ws` came from ITS OWN global install cache (378 entries, confirmed with
// --install=disable: ws still resolves, with no ws anywhere on disk and no node_modules above the repo).
// diffing feature-by-feature: EXACTLY ONE differs. `ws`, and Bun ships it BUILT IN --
// Bun.resolveSync("ws") returns a bare name rather than a file path, and a real WebSocketServer LISTENS under
// Bun with zero packages installed. *** AND THAT ONE DIFFERENCE IS NOT COSMETIC. *** server.js line 116 does
// `try { require("ws") } catch` and falls back to a NULL-OBJECT WebSocket server -- the bridge boots either
// way, and without ws live push is SILENTLY off. So on a box with no ws package, switching to Bun turns live
// updates on, which is plausibly the exact thing Keith noticed as "something was not working" running in
// reverse -- Node without ws installed would have been the degraded state, not Bun. *** THE PAGE ASKS THE BOX
// RATHER THAN TELLING IT. *** The runtime readout, the dependency table and the surface comparison all read
// live from /runtime and a new /runtime/surface route, so the page is never wrong about the NEXT box it runs
// on. The Bun button is disabled when bun is not on PATH, because preferring an absent runtime fails by doing
// NOTHING -- the launcher falls back to Node and the setting looks like it worked -- which is exactly the
// shape of "I switched it and something was off". *** THE SAFETY LINE: /runtime/surface TAKES A RUNTIME FROM
// AN ALLOWLIST, NEVER A COMMAND FROM THE QUERY STRING. *** Two fixed spellings, one fixed script
// (tools/ship/bunSurface.mjs); a route that ran what it was told would be a remote shell wearing a
// diagnostic's clothes, and this box exposes a tunnel. Three probes: let the query string choose the runtime
// name, enable the Bun button unconditionally, or drop the page from the registry -- each goes red. *** AND MY
// OWN GATE HIT THE TRAP THIS TREE ALREADY KNOWS, INVERTED. *** The page's prose originally read
// "ai-bridge/*Bridge.js" -- plain text, a glob in a sentence -- and noComments's lexer read that /* as a block-
// comment OPENER regardless of context, swallowing 6KB of the file and failing every check on a page that was
// correct. Fixed twice: the prose was reworded so no future tool inherits the landmine, and the gate is scoped
// to the <script> block rather than the whole HTML document.
// v3968 -- *** THE STUB-EMITTER, BUILT FIRST AND MEASURED BEFORE THE READ SIDE, WHICH IS WHY THE READ SIDE
// WOULD HAVE BEEN WORTHLESS. *** Arbor (RUC-NLPIR) propagates lessons from failed experiments to later ones;
// its actual mechanism is DISTILLATION, not retrieval -- an LLM abstracts each result and writes it into
// ancestor nodes, so a child inherits context for free. SweK already has the distilled half and better:
// okf/claims carries 241 hand-authored Prediction/Why/Measured/Kill-condition files, and brain/patchnotes has
// one whose entire value is the line "Two earlier obvious fixes were tried and measured INSUFFICIENT
// (documented so they're not re-attempted)". What it does not have is a corpus for THIS subsystem: MEASURED,
// zero of those 241 claims name a single RL environment, across twenty-eight files in brain/rl. So the read
// step was not built -- retrieval against an empty corpus is theatre -- and the writer went first.
// *** AND IT MUST NOT WRITE INTO okf/claims/, WHICH IS GENERATED. *** tools/okf/emitOKF.mjs opens with
// fs.rmSync(outDir, {recursive:true}) and rebuilds every claim from predictions.html; a stub there would
// survive until the next emit and vanish -- a corpus that LOOKS like it is accumulating and is not, which is
// the worst available failure. The machine corpus is ~/.voxelbridge/brain-lessons.jsonl: outside the tree,
// unswept by the packer, and no git churn on every training run. *** THREE DEFECTS, ALL FOUND BY RUNNING IT. ***
// (1) `require` DOES NOT EXIST IN .mjs and every fs call sat inside `try { require("fs") } catch { return
// false }` -- the first real run drove the real watchdog to three stalls and a NaN revert, wrote NOTHING, and
// reported nothing wrong. "Never throws" is the right contract for instrumentation and it converted total
// failure into silence, so health() exists now and the gate asserts through it: A RECORDER THAT CANNOT SAY
// WHETHER IT RECORDED IS NOT INSTRUMENTATION. (2) NINE ROWS FOR THREE PLATEAUS -- forty real ES steps on
// dockEnv produced nine stall records, three at bestScore -127.7566, three at -60.8869, three at -45.2518. The
// watchdog is correct (it re-kicks every `patience` steps while the score sits still); a corpus whose row
// count scales with RUN LENGTH is one nobody reads twice, which would have killed the read step for a reason
// having nothing to do with retrieval. Consecutive events at an unchanged score now fold into one record
// carrying its repeat count and iteration span. (3) THE BROWSER'S RECORDS CLAIMED TO BE NODE'S: brain-lab.html
// POSTs to /brain/lessons, the route re-validates through makeRecord, and makeRecord recomputed `runtime` from
// the SERVER -- three records made by Chromium landed stamped "node 22.22.2", the exact misattribution that
// field's own comment warns about. An incoming runtime is kept. *** WHAT IT ACTUALLY ACCUMULATES, MEASURED: ***
// three environments x 40 steps = NINE records, exactly three per env, one per genuine plateau. All nine are
// `stalled-kicked` -- reverted-nan never fired in real training, so the corpus is single-flavoured today. Two
// findings the read side will need: scores are NOT comparable across environments (dockHazard plateaus near
// -1901 where hunt plateaus near -31), and the useful signal is the REPEAT COUNT rather than the score -- six
// kicks over iters 12-27 is a wall, one kick at iter 7 is a blip, and they look identical without it.
// The wrapper returns the watchdog's own verdict untouched, asserted by comparing wrapped and unwrapped
// sequences: the instrument must not become part of the experiment.
// v3969 -- *** THE READ SIDE, AND THE CADENCE GAP TURNED OUT NOT TO BE ABOUT A TIMER. *** loop-engineering's
// pattern set is seven scheduled loops; six of them SweK already covers, several more rigorously (gate-driven
// runtime verification against their CI-log reading; a changelog written AT the bump against their lagging
// daily scan). The one that looked like a real gap was "nothing runs on a cadence" -- and the actual defect was
// narrower and worse: THE THINGS THAT ALREADY RUN CONTRIBUTED NOTHING. createWatchdog is used in exactly ONE
// place in this tree, brain-lab.html, a page a person must open; the gates that train on every ship round
// (dock, dock-hazard) call dockPolicy.trainDockES, which has no watchdog at all. A scheduler would have been
// the wrong fix for runs that were already happening. trainDockES gets an OPT-IN onIter observer instead --
// with no hook the branch is never taken, and both gate verdicts are byte-identical before and after, checked
// rather than assumed. One ship round now writes six lesson records without anybody opening a page.
// *** AND THE READER RANKS BY REPEAT COUNT, NOT SCORE, WHICH IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. ***
// v3968 measured the plateaus: dockEnv -127..-45, dockHazardEnv -1901..-1323, huntEnv -96..-32. These are
// environment-specific reward sums with no shared zero, so a reader sorting by score would put every
// dockHazard lesson above every hunt lesson forever AND CALL THAT RELEVANCE -- ranking by environment while
// reporting importance. The repeat count means the same thing everywhere: six kicks over iters 12-27 is a
// wall, one kick at iter 7 is a blip, and they are indistinguishable by score. A related environment is
// offered SEPARATELY and marked, never blended: dockEnv shares a policy shape with dockHazardEnv and not a
// reward scale, and merging them is how retrieval starts producing confident nonsense. *** THE GATE'S OWN
// FIXTURE WAS THE LAST THING WRONG, AND ONLY A PROBE FOUND IT. *** It put the worst score on the longest hold,
// so score-ranking and repeat-ranking produced the SAME list -- swapping the comparator for a score sort passed
// cleanly TWICE. A fixture where the wrong answer looks like the right one tests nothing; the longest-held
// plateau now carries the BETTER score, and the probe goes red. Three outcomes that all read as "no lessons"
// are kept distinct on purpose -- an empty corpus, a corpus with no match, and a hit -- because each wants a
// different response and only one of them means the reader is working. The brief is PRINTED, never fed back
// into the trainer: a retrieval step that silently changed hyperparameters would make every run
// un-reproducible from its own source.
// v3970 -- *** THE LESSON PATTERN, WIRED INTO THE PHYSICS LAB'S MOST EXPENSIVE GATE FAMILY. *** Seven separate
// files build the "kh" (Kelvin-Helmholtz) device with their own independent parameter sweeps -- run length
// (khConvergence, ~5min), viscosity (khGrowthKey, ~6.5min), box resolution (khMichalke, ~5.5min) -- and none of
// them could see what another had already found did not settle. Two of the three ALREADY hand-compute a
// "did this settle" verdict, by hand, in their own headers: khConvergence's ratio test (a converged quantity
// has every step-to-step ratio near 1; theirs jumps 3.9x) and khMichalke's non-monotone test (refinement moves
// the mode ladder rather than densifying it, so the error against Michalke SCATTERS -- 11.7%, 33.8%, 64.7%,
// 64.7% -- rather than shrinking). *** SO THE GATE'S OWN VERDICT IS OBSERVED, NOT RECOMPUTED, SAME RULE
// recordingWatchdog FOLLOWS ON THE RL SIDE. *** brain/rl/lessons.mjs gains recordSweepFinding(): a caller hands
// it a short series of {x, y, settled} points -- settled already decided by the gate's own comparison -- and it
// writes ONE lesson per sweep, keyed by how many transitions failed to settle, under event "sweep-unsettled" (a
// third event name, not folded into the watchdog's "stalled-kicked"/"reverted-nan" vocabulary, because a
// different producer deserves its own word). A sweep where every point settles writes NOTHING -- the same
// discipline as RECORDED_EVENTS: a corpus logging every clean sweep is a run log, not a lesson. *** THE BRIEF'S
// WORDING HAD TO BECOME EVENT-AWARE, AND ONLY BUILDING IT SURFACED THAT. *** lessonsBrief hardcoded "plateau
// ... N stall window(s)" for every record; a sweep finding is the OPPOSITE shape -- a sequence that SCATTERED,
// never one that sat still -- and describing a scatter as a plateau would send a reader looking for the wrong
// failure. It now reads "did not settle over <axis>: N of M step(s) jumped" for sweep-unsettled records.
// khConvergence and khMichalke now print the kh brief before spending their four-to-six minutes, and record
// their own verdict after; khGrowthKey reads but writes nothing, because its own check is a TREND ASSERTION
// (growth rate must rise as viscosity falls) rather than a stall -- there is no "did it settle" verdict for a
// gate that is testing whether physics behaved as expected. All THREE gates were re-run in full, unwired-vs-
// wired, and every verdict is byte-identical (khConvergence 4/4 PASS at ~4m52s, khGrowthKey 10/10 PASS at
// ~6m29s, khMichalke's real numbers matched its own header exactly). recordSweepFinding's converged-sweep
// guard was probed by DELETING it rather than merely disabling the write: the function does not degrade, it
// CRASHES on bad[bad.length-1] with an empty array, which is stronger evidence the guard is load-bearing than
// a graceful failure would have been.
// v3971 -- *** THE MPM SELF-CONVERGENCE TRAP, PROMOTED FROM A COMMENT INTO A CLAIM. *** Keith asked whether
// v3970's kh lesson pattern applies to MPM; it does not for the reason given -- MPM is 13 SEPARATE devices, not
// one device swept by several gate files, and the one that DOES sweep (mpmRefineBind, self-convergence under
// refinement) runs in 2.6 SECONDS. recordSweepFinding exists to save re-discovering a multi-MINUTE finding;
// there is nothing expensive here to avoid re-running, so wiring the corpus in would have been the pattern's
// SHAPE without its REASON. What mpmRefineBind.mjs's own header already had was better than a stall record: a
// methodological trap, discovered, measured and fixed at v3802/v3846/v3851, documented in prose and nowhere
// else. *** TWO INDEPENDENT WAYS A REFINEMENT RATIO LOOKS CONVERGED WHILE MEASURING NOTHING. *** Route one:
// holding a fixture's floor at a fixed CELL COUNT instead of a fixed WORLD HEIGHT moves the floor every
// refinement level -- a different problem each time -- and the sequence 1.1517/0.5840/0.3027 STILL CONVERGES,
// ratio 2.018, because it is converging to ZERO as the floor goes to zero. Route two, found lifting the same
// study to 3D: sampling com.y mid-collapse (t=0.5s, peak speed 3.9) gives ratio 0.045, and forcing that same
// under-resolved sample STILL PRODUCES A RATIO rather than refusing to answer -- a transient converges tidily
// too, on the wrong number. Both are now DRIVEN in mpmRefineBind-selfcheck.mjs (the cellfloor arm and the
// baseSteps:120 arm reproduce each trap on purpose) rather than merely described, and both are refused before
// the real ratio (1.151674/1.371714/1.473512, order 1.11) is trusted: a fixture-invariance number under
// tolerance, and restedAtSample true. *** THE CLAIM IS FILED AS GENERAL, NOT MPM-SPECIFIC, BECAUSE THAT IS
// WHAT IT IS. *** Any future self-convergence study anywhere in the roundhouse inherits both failure modes for
// free the moment it checks a ratio without first asking whether its fixture held still across levels and
// whether its observable was at rest when sampled. Added to predictions.html's SETTLED claims (241, was 240) --
// the actual source; okf/claims/*.md is generated from it and would have been wiped by the next emit, the same
// rule v3968 already established for the machine-written lesson corpus. Verified: claimsGate parses it with no
// problems, claimsGate-selfcheck/okf-selfcheck/okfConsume-selfcheck all pass, and the rendered claim file in a
// test emit carries the real numbers, not a summary of them.
// v3972 -- *** THE LESSON CORPUS, WIRED INTO THE MOST EXPENSIVE GATE IN THE TREE -- AND ONE OF ITS THREE
// SWEEPS DELIBERATELY REFUSED. *** Keith asked which OTHER gates fit v3970's recordSweepFinding pattern. The
// device-family survey I started with was the wrong axis: what put kh in and MPM out was never "how many gate
// files build this device", it was (a) does the gate hand-compute a settle verdict over a swept axis and (b)
// does re-discovering that verdict cost real minutes. So the whole tree was ranked by RECORDED RUNTIME out of
// gate-timings.json instead -- 1113 gates timed, only 15 over 100s -- and the expensive end read directly.
// THE ANSWER WAS IN physics/sph, WHICH THE DEVICE SURVEY COULD NOT SEE because levelClaim is not a roundhouse
// device at all. physics/sph/levelClaim-selfcheck.mjs is the single most expensive gate in the tree (646.9s
// recorded; its own header measured over 1200s on one core) and it hand-rolls the transitions-only monotone
// test TWICE plus a stationarity test, all three of which died with the process. *** WHAT IS WIRED: TWO OF
// THREE. *** Section 2's levelling series over settle time (300:11.045 -> 900:1.980 -> 1800:0.000 ->
// 3000:0.000; a step is unsettled when the spread ROSE, which is the reversal its ordering check already
// forbids) and section 4's tilt stationarity (3000 against 6000 steps, the 5% drift verdict the gate already
// renders). Both verdicts are OBSERVED, never recomputed -- the same rule recordingWatchdog follows -- so the
// corpus and the gate's own PASS lines cannot disagree. *** WHAT IS REFUSED, AND WHY IT IS THE POINT: *** the
// spread-against-tilt rows (0deg:0.0000 5deg:0.0455 10deg:0.8188 20deg:2.1845) look like the best candidate in
// the file -- four points, its own monotone loop, identical shape -- and have the OPPOSITE SEMANTICS. That
// sweep is a LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE: the spread is SUPPOSED to rise with the angle, so its failure is "the
// statistic went DEAF", not "the value would not settle". recordSweepFinding writes the event
// "sweep-unsettled" and the reader renders it as "did not settle over <axis>"; filing a discrimination failure
// under that sentence would send the next reader hunting a convergence failure that never happened. v3970
// learned this one size down (wording a scatter as a plateau "would send whoever reads it looking for the
// wrong failure"). THE CORPUS HAS NO WORD FOR THIS SWEEP YET, SO IT GETS NO RECORD RATHER THAN THE WRONG ONE.
// *** AND A DOCUMENTED REFUSAL THAT NOTHING ENFORCES IS A COMMENT. *** lessons-selfcheck now pins the count at
// EXACTLY TWO calls, forbids a tiltDeg axis, and requires the reasoning to still be present in the prose -- so
// adding a third call goes red and the next editor has to read why before widening it. *** THE WRITE PATH IS
// DRIVEN, NOT INSPECTED, BECAUSE A GREEN GATE WRITES NOTHING. *** levelClaim passes, so its real sweep is
// silent -- which makes a silent wiring bug indistinguishable from correct behaviour at run time, and source
// inspection can only prove the call is THERE, not that the boolean handed to it means what the gate means. So
// the mapping is replayed against levelClaim's OWN measured series: confirmed silent (including the
// 0.000 -> 0.000 tail, where a flat step is SETTLED, not a reversal -- `!(0 > 0)` is true, and getting that
// backwards would file the tree's most expensive gate as failing every run), then confirmed to land exactly
// one record with repeats=1 once a reversal is planted. Seven new checks, each broken individually to prove it
// bites: wrong env key, tilt axis smuggled in, a third call added, refusal reasoning deleted, try/catch
// stripped, settled polarity inverted, planted reversal flattened. *** THE codeOnly TRAP, HIT AGAIN AND
// WRITTEN DOWN THIS TIME. *** codeOnly() blanks comments AND THE CONTENTS OF STRING LITERALS, so the
// lessonsBrief("sph-level") check silently matched nothing and passed for the wrong reason until it was driven.
// Each check now uses the right view of the file -- codeOnly for counting calls, noComments for reading a key
// name, prose for grading the comment -- and that reasoning is written into the check rather than rediscovered.
// STATED LIMIT: the replay COPIES levelClaim's mapping rather than importing it, so it grades the intended
// semantics, not the live expression; sharing one declaration is a refactor of a 646s gate and is named for a
// deliberate future round instead of done inline. env is "sph-level", not a registry device name, because
// levelClaim is not a roundhouse device -- the "sph" family token is what will put it in one family with
// packingTransfer-selfcheck (186.6s, and its section already asserts a series that "never settles"), which is
