// v4033 -- *** THREE AVATAR CAMERAS THAT HAD NEVER HAD A RENDERED FRAME CHECKED, ALL FOUND BY CHASING ONE REAL BUG REPORT. *** Keith: universal-viewer.html#robotface showed a robot "cut off at the waist... no legs." face/avatarStage.js's avatarModel() scaled the mesh from rawBBox -- RobotExpressive's UNSKINNED bind pose, ~0.026 units tall -- while the GPU had already skinned it to its true ~4.5-unit pose BEFORE uModel applies (v4032's own candidate fix had scoped itself to camera framing only, on the record, because no working render existed yet to check the second half against). MEASURED: a headless render of avatarstage.html?glb=RobotExpressive before this fix shows the diorama's pet llama alone, correctly scaled, with NO ROBOT VISIBLE AT ALL -- fixed to source scale from posedBBox, same as the framing half already did. face/robotFaceAvatar.js's _updateRootFollow() eased the camera toward joint 0's ABSOLUTE world position on the assumption -- never measured -- that RobotExpressive's in-place clips keep it near the origin. It does not: joint 0 sits at (-0.003, 2.370, -0.021). Fixed to track DISPLACEMENT from a captured baseline instead, so an in-place clip contributes zero regardless of where its root bone happens to live, and genuine locomotion (the CesiumMan case this was built for) still accumulates normally. blob-avatar.html's orbit camera shipped at dist:3.0 against a figure whose balls span roughly +-1.5 world units -- so close the metaball surface filled the whole canvas; raised to 6.5 (verified across idle/wave/dance poses). NEW gate, tools/ship/avatarFraming-selfcheck.mjs: renders all three headlessly and measures non-background pixel coverage against an ADAPTIVE per-page background colour (three pages, three different backgrounds) -- a coverage RANGE, not a pixel-exact baseline, because animation timing and autonomous reactions make an exact match the wrong tool. Caught its own gap live: the robotFaceAvatar render check's first threshold (0.08) was too loose to catch the root-follow sabotage on its own (broken renders measured 0.13, comfortably above it) -- only the paired source check caught it, and the threshold was tightened to 0.17 using the measured broken/fixed ranges rather than left as a guess. All three sabotaged live and confirmed caught, then restored byte-identical. Also this round: ui/avatarSwitch.js's server.html avatar-corner cycle gained two named slots -- Keith: "RobotExpressive can be choice 4 on Server.html. and we can have StickWoman be choice 3" -- and swapped facemuscles for gauges3000.html (Keith: "swap out the gauges and avatar scene, and swap in the WebGPU gauges and avatar we already made") at the explicit last position, which gauges3000.html did not support embedding for until now (?embed=1, same pattern as blob-avatar.html's v3656 fix). universal-viewer.html's #robotface catalog id -- and RobotExpressive.html's own demo:title meta, which would have overwritten a CATALOG-only rename on the next auto-discovery pass -- were both years-stale ("Robot Avatar" / "Generated robot face"): renamed to #robotexpressive and "RobotExpressive Avatar" in both places at once, the two-copy trap this tree keeps finding avoided by fixing the writer AND the thing that reads it back. The test_rig and RobotWoman avatar-list labels ("test_rig (Robot Man)", "RobotWoman") were renamed to match this tree's own existing convention elsewhere (avatarstage.html's orientation-map and simulation/PipAvatar.js already call these "Stick Man" / "Stick Woman") -- StickMan (test rig) and StickWoman. And server.html gained a "Pipeline Routes" button (universal-viewer.html's Pipeline mode gained a ?mode=pipeline deep-link so the button can open straight into it), positioned exactly where asked: right after "Open SweK Engine", then a forced line break, so "Avatar mode" is always first on the next row.
// v4032 -- *** THE FLOOR ATLAS COULD NOT REACH ITS OWN DATA, AND THE PAGE ACCOUNTABILITY REGISTER WAS READING AN EMPTY DIRECTORY BY DESIGN. *** floor-atlas.html fetched "/roundhouse/" + name for its submitted bench reports; that URL prefix is claimed by ai-bridge/roundhouseBridge.js's agent API, whose owns() matches ANY path starting with it and whose handle() answers "unknown-route" 404 to anything it does not recognise -- so every request for a raw report was swallowed before serveStaticFile ever got a turn. CONFIRMED LIVE against a real running server: 404 on /roundhouse/magmap-bench.json, 200 on the same file at /tools/roundhouse/magmap-bench.json, which is where ai-bridge/androidPeerBridge.js's /android/submit endpoint actually writes it. tools/render-qa/deviceOwed.mjs had the same species of bug from the other direction: its receivedKinds() defaulted to ai-bridge/fleet, a directory nothing has ever written a report to (it does not even exist -- ai-bridge/fleetBridge.js owns /fleet/announce, an unrelated peer join/leave broadcast with no JSON files of its own). Both defaults made "no device reports have been folded yet" / "received kinds: NONE" true unconditionally, for every device, forever -- which is exactly the silence the three-state RENDERED/VERDICT-OWED/VERDICT-IN register exists to replace with a real answer, reached here by a wrong URL and a wrong default directory rather than a missing file. FOUND because Keith pasted a real swek-magmap-bench report from a Mac (Intel gen-8 GPU) -- the FIRST real device submission this atlas has ever received: wg128-shared confirmed fastest (1.21x speedup, 4.3ms vs the 5.2ms baseline) on a second, different Intel generation from the gen-9 rig that set the v3965 default, and the measured floor (worst relative diff 3.79e-6) lands at 2.64x margin under the shipped 1e-5 tolerance -- comfortable, not tight. The report was folded into tools/roundhouse/floor-atlas.json exactly as the live endpoint would have (same foldReport() call androidPeerBridge.js makes on every /android/submit), and magmap-bench.html now correctly reads VERDICT-IN instead of VERDICT-OWED. render/renderAccountability-selfcheck.mjs's "no verdict received" case, which had been calling audit({}) against the real repo tree with no dir override, moved to a sandboxed tmpdir so a future real submission cannot flip that assertion by accident the way this one nearly did unnoticed -- plus a NEW check that plants a marker directly in the real default directory with NO dir override at all, so a regression of the default itself (not just the read mechanism a sandboxed test would still exercise) fails loudly. tools/roundhouse/floorAtlas-selfcheck.mjs gained a fetch-path check that did not exist before this round: source-level proof that floor-atlas.html's fetch prefix cannot collide with roundhouseBridge's PREFIX, with the bridge's own owns() pattern read from source rather than assumed. Both new checks sabotaged (reverting the fetch prefix, reverting the default directory) and confirmed to fail loudly, then restored byte-identical. Also this round (unversioned patches folded in here, none previously given an ENGINE_VERSION line): knobLiveness.mjs got a main block over its own reportLines() and a reportingTools.mjs row, so graveyard-selfcheck reads 90 orphaned utilities against a 90 baseline, green, without the baseline being touched. Six pages created after the v3936 registerResidue ceiling was set (lensing.html and stellar.html into cosmic, ecology.html into matter, cartpole.html and reactor.html into physicslab, webgpu-llm.html into systools) were placed by MECHANISM rather than filename -- lensing is general relativity, not optics; cartpole carries LQR/Riccati with no policy or brain code anywhere in it -- and registerResidue-selfcheck moved from 47 back to the 41 ceiling exactly. And webgpu-llm.html's download door shipped (ui/localModelRun.js): Keith reported "i do not see a option to download/install," and the page had in fact been built to fetch nothing, on purpose, because v4007's whole point was answering the pre-flight question before the gigabyte. downloadGate() calls verdictFor() rather than restating its rules, opens on MAYBE with the unknowns shown rather than swallowed (v3103 runs both ways: unknown is not yes, and unknown is not no either), and preflightRepo() resolves config.json before one weight byte is requested. Building its gate hit this tree's codeOnly/noComments trap a seventh time: $("genBox") is a string literal that codeOnly blanks to $(""), so the check could never have matched what it claimed to check.
// v4031 -- *** A SHARED LEXER WAS SILENTLY BLIND ON 180 FILES, AND THE TWO THINGS BUILT ON TOP OF IT BOTH FOUND ME OUT. *** THE FIX: tools/ship/sourceScan.mjs's codeOnly()/noComments() had no concept of a regex literal. ai-bridge/gpuBrainBridge.js line 269 carries .replace(/^[\"']|[\"']$/g, \"\") -- a quote INSIDE a character class -- so the scanner spuriously opened STRING mode there and hunted for a matching quote through the rest of the file. Everything after that line was garbage to every caller, SILENTLY: a gate hunting a forbidden pattern past line 269 found nothing, not because nothing was there but because the lexer had stopped reading code. MEASURED tree-wide: 180 of 3198 js/mjs files desynced the same way, including tools/ship/proseAudit.mjs -- which sourceScan-selfcheck's own v3681 section already named as a casualty and DOCUMENTED rather than fixed. Now regex-literal aware: the \"is this a regex or division\" heuristic is LIFTED FROM prose()'s own stripToComment() rather than invented, and EXTENDED with the expression-starting keywords (return/typeof/case/new/throw/...) because stripToComment's punctuation-only form would misread `return /^x/` and \"return /\" occurs as literal text in 46 files here. The body scan is CHARACTER-CLASS AWARE (a / inside [...] does not close the literal) and has a SAFETY VALVE: a literal newline before any close means the / was misjudged, so the caller falls back to treating it as a plain character -- a wrong guess can never cost more than the one / it was wrong about, never a cascade. stripToComment now uses the shared pair, so THREE independently-drifting regex heuristics in one file became one. Tree-wide desyncs 180 -> 0. Section 4 of sourceScan-selfcheck was retitled from \"WHAT THIS CANNOT SAY\" -- leaving a gate asserting a limit that no longer exists is the stale-claim trap under a different mechanism. Five sabotages bite. SECOND BUILD -- fx/dither.js: ordered dithering (Bayer 8x8), lifted from lenxism/dither, whose ALGORITHM is what transfers (none of its React/Canvas2D code is here, and its Floyd-Steinberg half deliberately is not either: error diffusion is sequential and has no fragment-shader form, which is named rather than left for someone to rediscover). THE MATRIX IS GENERATED from the Bayer block recurrence and THE GLSL AND WGSL ARE GENERATED FROM THAT SAME ARRAY -- there is no second copy of 64 constants for v3527's rule to bite, and the gate asserts the digits appear NOWHERE in the source. The effect is MEASURED including the half that gets worse: the local 8x8 block mean tracks a slow 8-bit ramp 15.4x more closely (4.6x on a milder ramp, and it helps MOST where banding is worst, which is the mechanism's own prediction), while PER-PIXEL error gets 1.33x WORSE because dithering adds no information -- it moves error out of the low frequencies the eye integrates. COMPILED AND RENDERED ON A REAL GPU (Chromium 141/SwiftShader), not string-checked: a half-level column produces exactly the alternating 76,77 stipple down the 8-row Bayer period, and a column 0.99 of the way to a level is correctly left alone. WIRED into wormholeNebula as OPT-IN (uDitherLevels, 0=off), and the default is PROVEN unchanged rather than asserted: against the pre-wiring shader, 0 of 65536 bytes differ both with dither explicitly off AND with the uniform NEVER SET -- so an existing caller that has never heard of dithering gets exactly the pixels it got before, and the verified wormhole+nebula shadow test still passes. The WGSL struct did not grow: ditherLevels took the pad slot. THIRD BUILD -- brain/rl/surprise.mjs: prediction-error-as-anomaly-flag, the idea (not the code) from lucas-maes/le-wm. SURPRISE SAYS WHEN TO DISTRUST THE POLICY; IG SAYS WHY IT ACTED -- and IG on an out-of-distribution state returns a confident, axiom-satisfying, meaningless attribution, so the pairing is not decoration. explainIfSurprising() spends IG's 64 gradient evaluations only where surprise's ONE forward pass says the state is worth explaining: MEASURED 93.9% of gradient evaluations saved on a stream with 5% anomalies. The threshold is the empirical quantile, never a constant, fitted and estimated on DISJOINT HALVES; MEASURED 1.10% held-out false-positive rate against a 1% target, and detection rises monotonically 0.9% -> 100% with the size of a physics change, with a drift below the model's own noise floor correctly NOT claimed. An UNDERTRAINED forward model is REFUSED rather than silently used -- the headroom check compares the model's error against the data's own spread and, when there is no room to be surprised in, score() declines to flag even a wildly deviant input, because a confident verdict from a detector that cannot detect would be believed. *** THE GATES CAUGHT THEIR OWN AUTHOR FOUR TIMES THIS ROUND. *** (1) dither's load-bearing \"constants are generated, not pasted\" check asked whether ${BAYER8_LIST} appeared ANYWHERE in the file -- which the still-generated WGSL satisfied while the GLSL was pasted, so that exact sabotage produced ZERO failures; it now asserts the digits are absent, per template. (2) The dither banding measurement first used a ramp that lands exactly on the quantisation levels and reported a 1.0x \"improvement\" at 8 bits -- a FIXTURE ARTEFACT that would have read as \"dithering does not help at 8 bits\". (3) surprise's double-dipping sabotage was CONFOUNDED (duplicating rows changes the fit sample SIZE as well as the disjointness) and, repeated over 25 seeds, the direction was not even consistent -- two successive drafts of that comment asserted a direction, in OPPOSITE directions, and both were wrong; the claim was withdrawn and the split is now asserted as the structural fact it verifiably is. (4) A comment inside a WGSL template literal used backticks around a word and ended the template. *** AND THE FINDING I WAS NOT LOOKING FOR: brain/rl/attribution.mjs -- MY OWN v4027 INTEGRATED GRADIENTS -- HAS BEEN UNWIRED FOR FOUR VERSIONS. *** A grep returned hits in brain.js and main.js and I read that as \"it is wired\"; every one of those is a CHANGELOG COMMENT I wrote about the module, and the only real import in the tree is its own selfcheck's. That is unwiredRegister.mjs's own header warning -- \"written from the import graph and the file's name rather than from RUNNING THE THING\" -- reproduced exactly, by someone who had just read it. attribution.mjs and surprise.mjs are both registered now with VERIFIED blockers (surprise's: a grep across brain/ for predictNext/nextState/forwardModel/dynamicsModel returns NOTHING -- this tree trains policies, not dynamics, and an env's step() IS the true dynamics so a model that is the system is never surprised by it). Actionable orphans 93 -> 91. NOT SHIPPED: graveyard-selfcheck is still red by ONE (91 vs a 90 baseline) on tools/roundhouse/knobLiveness.mjs, which arrived in a patch round with a page and gate registered in instruments.mjs but no importer -- and the baseline is NOT being bumped to make a number go green. The full tools/ship/selfchecks.mjs battery over the lexer fix was still running when this was committed; this commit preserves the work on the feature branch and does NOT merge to main.
// v1986 — deterministic repro: parse ?seed/?cam/?preset NOW and apply the seed BEFORE any generator reads
// worldSeed (applySeedEarly writes the same localStorage key synchronously). Camera is applied later, after
// the camera object + spawn logic exist. window.repro exposes link()/params for capturing the current spot.
const _reproParams = (typeof window !== "undefined") ? reproParams.parseReproParams() : { seed: null, cam: null, preset: null };
if (typeof window !== "undefined") {
    try { if (_reproParams.seed != null) localStorage.setItem("voxelengine.worldSeed_v1", String(_reproParams.seed | 0)); } catch {}
    reproParams.applySeedEarly(_reproParams);
    window.repro = {
        params: () => _reproParams,
        link: () => reproParams.buildReproLink(window.camera),
        presets: () => reproParams.listPresets(),
        copy: () => { const u = reproParams.buildReproLink(window.camera); try { navigator.clipboard.writeText(u); } catch {} console.log("[repro] " + u); return u; },
    };
}
if (typeof window !== "undefined") {
    window.ENGINE_VERSION = ENGINE_VERSION;
    try { document.title = "SweK Engine " + ENGINE_VERSION; } catch {}   // never stale — tracks the build
    window.engineVersion = () => ENGINE_VERSION;
    // v1507 — follow the server across updates. If the bridge comes back on a HIGHER version (an
    // update applied while this page was open — including a silent poller/fleet apply), reload onto
    // it. Only-on-higher avoids a false reload during the brief restart-down window. Combined with
    // the launcher skipping a duplicate tab on update, an open page becomes the one current page.
    try {
        const _loadedV = parseInt(((ENGINE_VERSION.match(/v?(\d+)/) || [])[1]) || "0", 10);
        if (_loadedV) setInterval(() => {
            fetch("/package/info", { cache: "no-store" }).then(r => r.json()).then(info => {
                const m = String((info && info.version) || "").match(/v?(\d+)/);
                const v = m ? parseInt(m[1], 10) : 0;
                if (v && v > _loadedV) { try { console.log("[swek] server is now v" + v + " (was v" + _loadedV + ") — reloading onto the new build"); } catch {} location.reload(); }
            }).catch(() => {});
        }, 20000);
    } catch {}
    // v969 — benign-noise classifier. These are "an OPTIONAL service isn't
    // running" or "benign skip" messages — expected, not failures. Debug views,
    // the console mirror, and the perf monitor use this to avoid raising an
    // alarm (red) for them. Extend window._benignPatterns to add more.
    window._benignPatterns = [
        /OllamaDiffuser|ollamadiffuser|:9000\/api\/(tags|health)/i,   // diffuser not running (optional)
        /\/ha\/states|502 \(Bad Gateway\)|Bad Gateway/i,              // Home Assistant not reachable (optional)
        /:8765\/assets|assets\/events|AssetLibraryPanel.*(event stream|SSE)/i, // asset server down (optional)
        /ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED|net::ERR_/i,                          // optional local service offline
        /no default export, skipped/i,                               // benign demo-loader skip
        /intro\.mp4.*404|404 \(Not Found\).*(intro|ogre_boat_carrier)/i, // missing optional media
        /AudioContext was not allowed to start/i,                    // benign until a user gesture
        /message channel closed before a response/i,                 // benign extension noise
        // v1611 — more known-benign noise so the avatar stays calm for "an optional thing isn't here" cases:
        /showcase\.json|404 \(Not Found\).*showcase/i,               // no showcase allowlist in this tree (dev)
        /Permissions policy violation|unload is not allowed/i,       // benign jQuery unload-handler warning
        /willReadFrequently/i,                                       // Canvas2D perf HINT, not an error
        /KPop LISTENER\] loaded undefined clips/i,                   // cosmetic listener log
        /comfyui\/system_stats|ComfyUI.*not reachable/i,             // ComfyUI not running (optional)
        /:1984\/api\/frame\.jpeg|go2rtc/i,                           // camera proxy off (optional)
    ];
    window._benignError = (text) => { try { return window._benignPatterns.some(re => re.test(String(text || ""))); } catch { return false; } };
    // v856 — detect version bump on load and fire engine:roundShipped
    // once. The v849 Twitch broadcast hook + v855 audio cue (feed
    // fanfare) listen for this. Suppresses repeat fires by storing
    // the last-seen version in localStorage. First load on a fresh
    // browser doesn't fire (no baseline to compare against).
    try {
        const KEY = "voxelEngine.lastShippedVersion";
        const last = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
        if (last && last !== ENGINE_VERSION) {
            // Defer dispatch until after the page is fully loaded so
            // listeners (window.twitch.announce, audio cues) are wired.
            setTimeout(() => {
                try {
                    window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("engine:roundShipped", {
                        detail: { round: ENGINE_VERSION, prev: last },
                    }));
                } catch {}
            }, 2500);
        }
        localStorage.setItem(KEY, ENGINE_VERSION);
    } catch {}
}

